data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique for modifying living organisms' genomes, based on a simplified bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1]\n- Cas9 nuclease is delivered into a cell with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), which allows the genome to be cut at a desired location [1]\n- In prokaryotes, CRISPR sequences derive from bacteriophage DNA and are used to detect and destroy similar phage DNA during later infections [2]\n- Gene editing makes targeted DNA changes through insertion, deletion, or substitution; CRISPR, zinc finger nucleases, and TALENs are examples [3]\n- Reported uses, per headlines only: a 2021 story on CRISPR opening possible treatment for a broad range of diseases [4], and a 2024 report that HIV in cell culture was eliminated using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]"}

